#!/usr/bin/env sh
# peaks-loop commit gate. Design + why-it-is-a-ratchet: .husky/peaks-gate.mjs
#
# Staged files only: a file you touch may not get worse, and a file that did
# not exist before must be clean outright. Files you did not touch are exempt,
# because 1178 of 1266 are currently unformatted and blocking on those would
# block every commit from the first one.
#
# Deliberately NOT here:
#   - tsc        whole-program; it cannot be scoped to a file list, so a
#                single-file commit would pay for the entire repo anyway.
#                It runs in CI: `npm run build` in .github/workflows/ci.yml
#                does the canonical typecheck (`tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`).
#                (This line said "it runs in the push gate" until slice B5,
#                2026-09-23. That was true then and is false now: the push
#                gate checks only the files being pushed, and no longer runs
#                a whole-program tsc.)
#   - unit tests ~7 min; CI runs the full suite in the `vitest + build` job.
#                The push gate runs only the tests the pushed diff implies
#                (`pnpm test:changed`, see .husky/pre-push).
#   - formatting OUTSIDE the lint scope, by the PER-FILE ratchet. `pnpm
#                format:check` covers src, tests, packages and scripts; the
#                per-file ratchet compares only the owner-decided lint scope
#                (src/** + packages/*/src/**, .husky/lint-scope.mjs, owner
#                decision 2026-10-03) and says so by name for every staged file
#                it drops ("outside the lint scope, not compared by the per-file
#                ratchet").
#                THE TWO SCOPES ARE MEANT TO BE DIFFERENT, and until rid-038 the
#                reason was written down nowhere. The per-file ratchet is a
#                PER-FILE RATCHET ("a file you touch may not be worse than its
#                own baseline row"), so it can only cover files that HAVE a
#                baseline row — the owned product boundary. Widening THAT
#                ratchet to tests/** + scripts/** would re-open an owner
#                decision, need rows for ~600 more files, and refuse every commit
#                touching an already-unformatted file (an absent row reads as
#                NEW FILE, which must be clean outright). So the wide check is
#                NOT narrowed to match this hook, and the per-file ratchet is
#                NOT widened to match the wide check.
#                THE WIDE CHECK IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF CHECK, which is how both
#                of those can be true at once. It is a SET ratchet over the SAME
#                four globs (package.json#scripts['format:check']): no per-file
#                row, one allowed SET of known names, and it may only shrink.
#                That set is EMPTY since 2026-10-17 — its names were each
#                collected by formatting the file the ratchet named — so over
#                those four globs its rule is literally "no unformatted file at
#                all", and an empty set can never regrow into a permission. It
#                needs no baseline row, so the objection above does not apply to
#                it, and that is the whole difference.
#                WHERE IT RUNS. Whole tree: the `format-check` job of
#                .github/workflows/ci.yml and the push gate's leg 1
#                (.husky/format-check-ratchet.mjs). STAGED FILES ONLY: this
#                hook, as .husky/gate/wide-format.mjs — because three files
#                reached a commit unformatted in one slice (2026-10-23) with
#                every commit-time gate green, and the push gate refused the
#                push each time, so the commit boundary did not hold. The
#                commit-time leg applies the wide rule to the staged files the
#                lint scope DROPS, and to nothing else: a staged file inside the
#                lint scope stays the per-file ratchet's business, and the
#                eslint leg is not widened by any of this. The two scopes stay
#                different, and a staged code file is now dropped by at most one
#                of them.
set -e
pnpm exec lint-staged
# Staged-parity leg. APPENDED, on purpose, and BEFORE the artifact-contract leg:
# it is cheaper (~250 ms vs ~610 ms for this session's commits, both measured
# 2026-10-12) and it is about what is being committed, which the legs after it
# presuppose. Design, the edges, and what it deliberately does not cover:
# .husky/peaks-gate-staged-parity.mjs (its decisions are in .husky/staged-parity/).
# Plain `node`, not tsx: it reads no `.ts`, so it needs no boot — a leg that
# spawned tsx would cost more than it saves.
node .husky/peaks-gate-staged-parity.mjs
# Artifact-contract leg. APPENDED, on purpose: the lint-staged leg above keeps
# its order and its behaviour, and this one is not a ratchet over staged files
# but a whole-session judgement, so it has nothing to sequence against.
# Design, why-it-refuses, and the exemption's known cost:
# .husky/peaks-gate-pipeline.mjs. It needs tsx because it reads the checker from
# source, never from the gitignored `dist/` (see that header).
node node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs .husky/peaks-gate-pipeline.mjs
